<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>preview</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>

    <!--
      The trim and AOT analyzers live in Directory.Build.targets, not here. See
      the comment there: this file is imported before the project sets
      TargetFramework, so a condition on it silently evaluates to false.
    -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Label="Package">
    <!--
      Explicit, and deliberately below 1.0. Without it every package is 1.0.0 by
      default, which is a promise this library has not made: P3 is not started
      and the tool-result surface changed twice after it was asserted stable.
      Consumers pin this, so it has to mean something.

      Raise it in the same commit as the change it describes, not afterwards. A
      version already on the feed is immutable and the publish job pushes with
      the skip-duplicate flag, so shipping a change without moving this
      publishes nothing and reports success: consumers keep restoring the old
      assembly and find out somewhere else entirely.
      `.github/version-guard.sh` is what stops that, and it runs on pull
      requests, so the failure arrives before a merge rather than after one.
      (The flag is named in prose because an XML comment cannot contain a
      double hyphen, which is how this file broke the build once already.)

      That guard does not watch this file, because this is where <Version> lives
      and watching it would make every bump a change demanding a bump. So a
      package property edited here — Authors, a new global property — is on you.
    -->
    <Version>0.2.0</Version>
    <Authors>wileyc427</Authors>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/wileyc427/AgentInfer</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/wileyc427/AgentInfer</PackageProjectUrl>
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <PackageTags>agents;llm;ai;source-generator;roslyn;openai;ollama;microsoft-extensions-ai</PackageTags>

    <!--
      The XML documentation, shipped rather than compiled and discarded. It is
      this project's API reference — there is no generated doc site — and
      without it a consumer hovering a method in their IDE gets the signature
      and nothing else.

      This one belongs HERE and not in Directory.Build.targets, which is the
      opposite of the trim analyzers two files over, and the reason is the same
      import order read from the other end. The SDK turns
      GenerateDocumentationFile into the DocumentationFile the compiler is
      actually passed during PROPS evaluation, long before Directory.Build.targets
      is read — but the switch that makes the XML-comment warnings fatal is read
      later, and does see it. Set it in targets and you get every CS1587 and
      CS1573 in the tree as a build error, and no .xml file in the package: the
      enforcement without the artifact. Verified by opening the nupkg, which is
      the only way to catch it.

      The cost of being early is that this cannot be scoped to what ships, since
      IsPackable is set in the project body and is not visible from here.
      Projects that ship nothing turn it off themselves, the same way they
      already turn off the trim analyzers — see the generator, the samples and
      the test projects.
    -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

    <!--
      CS1591 (missing XML comment on a public member) is suppressed, and it is a
      concession rather than a decision. Roughly fifty public members have no
      comment of their own — mostly overload families sitting under one shared
      remark, and interface implementations that inherit it — so enforcing it
      today fails the build on all of them at once.

      What is wanted is to write those comments and delete this line. It is here
      so that shipping the docs that DO exist is not blocked on the ones that do
      not.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>

    <!--
      A symbol package, so a consumer stepping into this library gets source
      rather than decompiled IL. Cheap: snupkg is a separate file that nobody
      restores unless their debugger asks for it.
    -->
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">
    <!--
      Normalizes the file paths baked into the PDB, which is what makes Source
      Link resolve them against the repository rather than against a runner
      directory that no longer exists. Only on CI: locally the absolute paths
      are the useful ones.
    -->
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild>true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
